 ref still meets that. The warning guards a mistake nobody asked about. Keep it if you want it, but the label is wrong. Its own \"if removed\" line (\"a mistyped ref is recorded silently\") describes a defensive concern.\n- **Decision ordering in `show-labels` (required → incidental).** `orderDecisions`, `compareRefs` and the bare-number forms of `refOf` do not appear in the task. The summary adds \"decisions are listed in ref order\" as a separate outcome. It is about 40 lines plus a test. It is a reasonable extra, but it is not required by the task.\n- **`plainTitle` in `label-core` (required → speculative, read).** The `1. Title` heading support is only exercised by synthetic tests (`'a plain 1. Title line…'`). None of the four real-report fixtures depend on it; they use `#` or bold headings. It is the source of the mislabel in missing risk 1, and its conservative rules add complexity (about 15 lines). Its value is unproven.\n- **`old-tests-adapt` (mechanical).** Accurate. The nested `start` behaviour it refers to is in `src/html/client.js` at HEAD~1, so it is not part of this diff. The group is a legitimate follow-on.\n- **`docs-refs` (supporting).** Accurate, but the README paragraph is very long. It is hard to read as documentation.\n\n### 3. Removal candidates\n\n- **`plainTitle` (in `label-core`).** Expected impact: a real report using `1. Title` headings (with blank lines, then bullets) would have those headings treated as items. Refs would shift. The four fixtures would still pass, since none uses this form. Dropping it makes labelling simpler and removes the verified mislabel below. The trade-off is that such headings would then count as items.\n- **Decision ordering and `decide-warn`.** Both can go without breaking the task. Expected impact: `reviews` and the page list decisions in time order, and a mistyped ref is recorded silently.\n\n### 4. Simplifications\n\n- `pageReviews` now sorts decisions by time and then re-orders them with `orderDecisions`, and the comment explains this. A single `orderDecisions` call would do if you only need the newest `MAX_PAGE_DECISIONS` by time first. This is minor.\n- The `[N]` / `N.M` / numbered-heading-as-bare-ref cases in `scan` add three branches. If the title rule is kept, a short table of rules in the header comment would be easier to check than the prose.\n\n### 5. Missing risks\n\n1. **Verified: a numbered finding with blank lines around it is mistaken for a heading.** I ran this:\n   ```\n   ## Bugs\n\n   1. Race in cache\n\n   - evidence: lines 4-9\n   - fix: lock it\n\n   2. Leak in pool\n\n   - evidence: line 12\n   ```\n   The output labels `evidence` as `[1.1]` and `fix` as `[1.2]`, leaves `Race in cache` unlabelled, and labels `evidence` under `Leak in pool` as `[2.1]`. So `--decide \"1.2 …\"` would name the fix rather than the race. The doc comment's rule (\"a blank line on each side, followed by a bullet\") matches this exactly, so it is accepted behaviour, not a bug in the code. But it is the common shape for a numbered finding that has sub-bullets, and it is the most likely way a reviewer's findings get the wrong refs. The author's risk list says \"can mislabel an unusual report\", which understates how ordinary this layout is. **Verified** by running `labelFindings`.\n2. **Refs are not stable across versions of the labeller (read).** A decision stores `1.2` as text. If the title rule changes in a later release, the same stored decision points at a different finding. The author should say the refs are only meaningful with the labeller that printed them.\n3. **Labelling runs on every `reviews` and html request (read).** Each report up to 256 KiB is rescanned on every render. The hostile-input test passes in about 110 ms, so this is fine for now, but it is worth knowing.\n4. **`decide-warn` reads every holder's reviews again (read).** It only runs when the finding starts with a ref, so the cost is small.\n\n**Checked and accurate:** the linear-time claim (the 50k-item and 200 KB-line test ran in 112 ms; the code reads as bounded per line); the idempotence claim (tests pass); the raw storage claim (`verdictOf` and record keys use the raw text; the test asserts the stored report is raw); the `--decide` never-refuses claim (the test checks it).\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
