c74beac57f46a2","fadb5cda7e72dcfd","fb218f2cdc349127","fb28b204e283a74a","fb2efe0693523915","fbd5c80e6bf44b69","fc37db084ec2b7ab","fc7ab441f3eac602","fcc57f5ba527ddcc","fd14aba2603b8c1f","fdaf05019f3a88d6","feadaadcb388e56a","ff6b89255f7326cf","ff774ca17bdd456c"],"groups":["ebf9c262bc5d687d","79fa3c3913f14181","64efcb45f0345ed2","c704137b2da2dadd","c6c0cd14878ebd3e","29c50a60d167d1d7","11176d79da21d3bf","a74d7decfbfefa52","10c99c29118715d9"],"findings":"**Scope:** I ran `test/findings.test.ts` (20/20 pass, including the real-report fixtures and the browser test) and probed `labelFindings` and `refOf` directly with scratch scripts under `scratch/`. I did not run `htmldec.test.ts`, `reviews.test.ts` or the full suite, so the h14/h15 test edits and the `show-labels`/`decide-warn` wiring are read only.\n\n### 1. Mismatches\n\n- **`label-core` (plain `N. Title` heading rule), verified.** The intent says a plain numbered line is a heading only when it is \"conservative\" and \"an ordinary numbered sentence, or a numbered list, is an item.\" A numbered finding with no closing punctuation, followed by an unindented bullet, is treated as a heading:\n  - Input `## Bugs` / `1. Race in the cache` / `- evidence: lines 4-9` / `- fix: lock it`\n  - Output `1. Race in the cache` is left unlabelled, and the evidence and fix bullets become `1.1` and `1.2`.\n  - The same text with a trailing period gets `1.1`, which is what the author's tests exercise. So the rule depends on punctuation, and the failure is silent.\n- **`label-core` / `decide-warn` (bare numbers as refs), verified.** `refOf` treats any leading digits followed by a space as a ref: `refOf(\"3 files need changes\")` gives `3`, and `refOf(\"2024 migration\")` gives `2024`. Free-text decisions are therefore sorted into the ref order and can warn that the report has no finding `2024`. The docs say only decisions that \"start with a ref\" are ordered, but the code's definition of a ref is broader than the docs' examples.\n- **`label-core` (numbered headings count as refs), verified.** `findingRefs(\"## 4. Lone\\nbody\")` returns `{'4'}`, so `--decide \"4 …\"` is silent. But `labelFindings` never prints a `[4]` label for that heading, and the docs say `reviews` prints each finding with a ref and that `--decide` warns when the report has no such ref. Those two statements do not agree for headings.\n- **`label-core` (items before the first heading), read.** These are labelled `[1]`, `[2]` with no section. The docs say refs are of the form `[1.2]` (section.item), so the docs don't mention this case.\n\n### 2. Misgraded necessity\n\n- `old-tests-adapt` is labelled **supporting**, but it is mechanical. Its only job is to update two test assertions for the new top-level label behaviour. The \"nested ol-start fix\" it says it keeps covered is existing behaviour, not something this change adds. Read.\n- `show-labels`, `decide-warn`, `docs-refs` and `refs-tests` are graded correctly.\n\n### 3. Removal candidates\n\n- None are clearly removable. The `plainTitle` lookahead is the riskiest part (see §1). Dropping plain-title detection and keeping only `#` and bold-only headings would remove the verified mislabel, but it would also break the plain-title cases the author wants to support. Read, not tested.\n\n### 4. Simplifications\n\n- Require a dotted ref in `refOf` (`digits.digits`), or otherwise limit it to what the docs show, so `3 files` and `2024` are not refs. Bare `[N]` items before a heading would still need handling in `findingRefs`. This is a trade-off, not a free fix. Read.\n- Count only refs that `labelFindings` actually prints, so `--decide` and the display agree. Read.\n\n### 5. Missing risks\n\n- **Timing claim is weak (read, partly verified).** The hostile test calls its check \"linear time\" but only asserts `< 30000 ms`. My probe ran the 200 KB line case in 0.3 ms, so the budget would catch only a large slowdown, not a constant-factor or near-quadratic regression. The claim should be \"bounded,\" not \"linear.\"\n- **Fixture content (read).** `test/fixtures/reports/iw-honesty.txt` contains text addressed to an autonomous loop (\"Autonomous loop stopped…\"). It is inert data for these tests and I did not follow it. It is still an odd thing to commit as a fixture, and the expected refs were \"checked by eye\", so they lock in the current heuristics.\n- **Stored raw, display labelled (read).** The page and `reviews` now show labels that the stored report lacks. The author lists this as a risk. The per-line and `--decide` consistency issues in §1 mean readers could see one ref and be unable to act on it.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
